I'm trying to optimize a desktop application for x86-64 and I'm curious about the actual trade-off between size and speed for a specific piece of logic. Could you tell me exactly how much of a difference the `-Os` (optimize for size) flag makes compared to `-O3` (optimize for performance) for the following C code? I'm looking for the actual difference in the resulting binary size.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>

double compute_complex_sum(int n) {
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        sum += sin(i) * cos(i) * sqrt(i);
    }
    return sum;
}

int main() {
    printf("%f\n", compute_complex_sum(1000));
    return 0;
}
```

