I'm trying to implement a custom iterator in Rust that holds a reference to its parent collection, and I'm running into a really frustrating lifetime issue. I've tried a few different ways of annotating the structs, but I keep getting a "borrowed value does not live long enough" error. 

I've written a snippet where the iterator is tied to the lifetime of the collection, but I'm not sure if this specific pattern of nested lifetimes will actually satisfy the borrow checker or if it's just logically flawed. Could you take a look at the code below and tell me if this actually compiles? I'm not sure if it's a version-specific issue with the compiler or a fundamental error in my logic.

```rust
struct MyCollection<'a> {
    data: Vec<&'a str>,
}

struct MyIter<'a, 'b> {
    parent: &'a MyCollection<'b>,
    index: usize,
}

impl<'a, 'b> MyIter<'a, 'b> {
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'b str> {
        if self.index < self.parent.data.len() {
            let val = self.parent.data[self.index];
            self.index += 1;
            Some(val)
        } else {
            None
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let text = "hello world";
    let coll = MyCollection { data: vec![text] };
    let mut iter = MyIter { parent: &coll, index: 0 };
    println!("{:?}", iter.next());
}
```

