I'm trying to optimize a small C program for an embedded target where flash space is extremely limited. I've written a utility that performs some basic CRC checksums and string manipulation, but I'm not sure which clang optimization flags will actually yield the smallest binary footprint for this specific logic.

Here is the code:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <string.h>

uint32_t crc32(const uint8_t *data, size_t length) {
    uint32_t crc = 0xFFFFFFFF;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        crc ^= data[i];
        for (int j = 0; j < 8; j++) {
            crc = (crc >> 1) ^ (0xEDB88320 & (-(crc & 1)));
        }
    }
    return ~crc;
}

int main() {
    const char *msg = "BloomEvals_Test_String";
    uint32_t result = crc32((const uint8_t *)msg, strlen(msg));
    printf("CRC32: %08X\n", result);
    return 0;
}
```

Between `-Os` and `-O3`, which one results in a smaller binary for this specific snippet? If you can, could you tell me exactly how much smaller the resulting executable is?

